WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.263
Sooner or later, two lines of work have to come together.

2
00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:09.360
Git gives you two ways to do it: merge and rebase.

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:14.029
You start a branch called feature, and make two commits on it, D and E.

4
00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:16.245
Meanwhile, main stays where it was.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.649
Now you switch to main, and run git merge feature.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.238
main gained no commits since feature split off from it.

7
00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:26.425
So git simply moves the main pointer forward, to E.

8
00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:27.863
It creates nothing new.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.785
That is called a fast-forward, and git prints exactly that.

10
00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:33.837
History stays one straight line.

11
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.378
Now a different story.

12
00:00:35.378 --> 00:00:39.261
While you worked on feature, someone added a commit C to main.

13
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.042
Both branches have new commits now, so a fast-forward is impossible.

14
00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:48.888
Git finds the merge base: the newest commit that both branches contain.

15
00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:49.980
Here, that is B.

16
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.611
Then it compares each side with the base.

17
00:00:53.611 --> 00:00:56.665
Say the base has a tax rate of 0.20.

18
00:00:56.665 --> 00:01:01.076
main changed it to 0.25, and feature still has 0.20.

19
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.439
Only main changed that line, so git takes 0.25.

20
00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:10.581
Without the base, git would see two different lines, and could not tell which side changed.

21
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:17.716
If both sides had changed that same line in different ways, that would be a conflict, and git would stop and ask you.

22
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:22.834
So git combines the two sets of changes into a new merge commit, M.

23
00:01:22.834 --> 00:01:24.984
It has two parents: C, and E.

24
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:26.196
main moves to M.

25
00:01:26.196 --> 00:01:30.779
The history shows both lines of work, and no existing commit changed.

26
00:01:30.779 --> 00:01:33.900
That is why a merge is safe on shared branches.

27
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:38.708
Rebase is the other way to bring your branch up to date.

28
00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:40.073
Same start as before.

29
00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:43.129
This time, on feature, you run git rebase main.

30
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.842
It works in three steps.

31
00:01:44.842 --> 00:01:49.829
First, it lists the commits on your branch that main does not have: D and E.

32
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.548
Second, it moves your branch to main's newest commit, C.

33
00:01:53.733 --> 00:01:57.895
Third, it applies your commits again, one at a time, on top.

34
00:01:57.895 --> 00:01:59.421
D prime, then E prime.

35
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:02.583
Each replayed commit has a new parent.

36
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:07.805
The parent is part of a commit's hash, so D prime is a new commit, with a new ID.

37
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.655
The old D and E still exist, but no branch points to them any more.

38
00:02:12.655 --> 00:02:14.115
Only the reflog does.

39
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:18.881
You end up with one straight line of history, as if you had started your work today.

40
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.054
Now the one rule that matters most.

41
00:02:22.054 --> 00:02:25.467
Do not rebase commits that other people already have.

42
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:29.198
Say a teammate fetched your branch before you rebased it.

43
00:02:29.198 --> 00:02:31.614
Their copy still holds the old D and E.

44
00:02:31.614 --> 00:02:34.092
Your history and theirs no longer match.

45
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:41.932
If they now merge your rebased branch, their history holds each of your changes twice, under the old IDs and the new ones.

46
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:44.271
Someone has to clean that up by hand.

47
00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:48.357
Rebasing your own branch, before anyone builds on it, is fine.

48
00:02:48.357 --> 00:02:50.240
That is how most teams use it.

49
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:55.744
A merge adds a commit with two parents, and it is safe on shared branches.

50
00:02:55.744 --> 00:03:02.411
A rebase copies your commits on top with new IDs: great for your own branch, never for commits others have.
